    "answer": {
      "short": "The numerator $m+n+m^2+n^2$ is even in every parity case, so the expression is an integer.\n\nWrite $m$ and $n$ as $2a$ or $2a+1$ and check the four cases.",
      "worked": "Let $N=m+n+m^2+n^2$, and let $a,b$ be integers.\n\nIf $m=2a$ and $n=2b$:\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=2a+2b+4a^2+4b^2\\\\N&=2(a+b)+4(a^2+b^2)\\end{align*}$$\nIf $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=2a+(2b+1)+4a^2+(2b+1)^2\\\\N&=2(a+b)+4(a^2+b^2)+4b+2\\end{align*}$$\nIf $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b$:\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=(2a+1)+2b+(2a+1)^2+4b^2\\\\N&=2(a+b)+4(a^2+b^2)+4a+2\\end{align*}$$\nIf $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b+1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=(2a+1)+(2b+1)+(2a+1)^2+(2b+1)^2\\\\N&=2(a+b)+4(a^2+b^2)+4a+4b+4\\end{align*}$$\nIn every case $N$ is even. Therefore,\n$$\\frac{m+n}{2}+\\frac{m^2+n^2}{2}=\\frac{N}{2}$$\nis an integer.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 132
  },
